description:
Follows docs/research/pipeline-cost-results.md, on the user's decision of 2026-10-08 to cut cost further once P118 landed. P118 measured the per-task cycle at 1.29-1.44 times plain like for like, and the phase overhead at 0.60, 0.75 and 1.15 against a budget of 1.00. The phase overhead is the larger remaining share. The user's priorities, in order:
1. measure what the phase review actually catches, then narrow it or make it conditional;
2. enforce the executor's one-line hand-back by mechanism rather than prose;
3. measure CLAUDE.md in each agent's start, and drop it where the brief already carries the rules.
The order is fixed: an offline measurement and design task first, from the recorded benchmark streams, with no paid call. Then the levers it recommends, each with its micro-test. Then one paid probe and the full benchmark, run by the orchestrator. A smaller model for low-risk tasks is the user's call and is out of scope. The cache TTL stays documented only, as decided on 2026-10-07. No lever may weaken a guarantee P118 holds.

tasks.description:
Measure and design, with no paid call, from the recorded benchmark streams: probe-C-1, cost-C-1, cost-C-2 and cost-C-3 at 1dd702f2, plus the arm A and arm B streams. The P118 results document names where they are. The result is a design section added to docs/research/pipeline-cost-results.md or a new docs/research document, with every figure beside the command that prints it. It covers:
(a) the phase overhead split per step: phase review dispatch, triage, phase gate, sign-off, landing and lock;
(b) a value test for the phase review: what each one found in those sessions, and whether any finding changed a graded result or the shipped tree;
(c) the executor's hand-back per cycle: its output tokens and the main loop's read of it;
(d) CLAUDE.md in each agent's start: bytes, tokens, and which of its rules the agent prompt or the brief already restates;
(e) a recommended set of levers with predicted per-task and per-phase ratios. Each lever names its mechanism, its micro-test, and the guarantee it must keep. Each estimate is labelled with its basis, and doubling any single estimate is shown not to break the target.
Fix the internal fault where tools/stream-cost.py misprices a follow-up stream read on its own: accept several streams of one session in one call, or refuse the follow-up read alone. Every new instrument case is shown red first.

outcome.technical:
Lever B. After `drive-phase.py submit` files a return, its last printed line tells the agent to hand back the line above as its whole reply. When `submit` refuses, the last line says to hand back the refusal verbatim. Both texts are STEPS entries (handback-filed, handback-refused), printed by handback_lines(code) after render() in run_submit. This covers the executor, the per-task reviewer and the phase reviewer's `submit --head`, every refusal submit() returns, and run_submit's two stderr usage refusals. Argparse's own errors are not covered. The hand-back sentence is removed from audit-executor.md and audit-reviewer.md. `tools/measure-context.py --gate` holds the executor at 7030/9000 and the reviewer at 6920/7000. No stop hook was added: the levers document prices one as a net cost.

Cases, inside the ds-block stage so the red helper can narrow to them:
- dh1: the filed line, then the instruction, with "return filed" once and the print within its byte bound;
- dh2, dh3: the refusal twins;
- dh4: only the refusal says verbatim;
- dh5: the phase reviewer's --head, filed and refused;
- dh6: neither prompt says hand back, and both name submit;
- ds2 now expects the two-line print.

Red-first: proved (ds-block at HEAD 8/15, with ds2 and dh1-dh6 red).

Mutation probe (scratchpad P119.3-g0k/mut.py), re-run by the orchestrator: exit 0, baseline green, five rows KILLED (no-line, always-filed, always-refused, not-verbatim, line-first).

Left for the next task in the brief's file: audit-lookup.py's briefs still say "hand back one line", so an agent now reads the instruction twice.

Observed: test_drive_phase.py takes about 225 s on its own, against the sweep's 300 s per-file cap. HEAD's copy takes about 230 s, so this change did not cause it. A loaded machine timed it out once.
